    "created_at": "2016-02-24T00:34:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-02-24T00:34:00Z",
    "body": "> What about all the other users though?\n\nThe \"sequential focus navigation starting point\" affects focus movement by pressing tab key so that non-keyboard users will not be affected.\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#sequential-focus-navigation\n\n> I would be okay with strongly encouraging this for certain scenarios, but we cannot require UX.\n\nAt this time, the \"[scroll to the fragment identifier](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#scroll-to-the-fragment-identifier)\" requires some of UX for pointing device users. In particular, the \"scroll to the fragment identifier\" requires that UAs make the target (#foo) visible (step 3 of algorithm).\n\n> Use the scroll an element into view algorithm to scroll target into view, with the align to top flag set. [CSSOMVIEW]\n\nBecause of this step, pointing device users are immediately able to click or tap the target or its siblings because the nodes are visible.\n\nOn the other hand, keyboard users are or are not immediately able to operate the target or its siblings depending on whether the target is focusable or not. This PR intends to solve this inconsistency.\n\nIn case the target is not focusable:\n1. A user opens https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#scroll-to-the-fragment-identifier\n2. \"When the user agent is required to scroll to the fragment identifier …\" will be displayed (step 3 of algorithm)\n3. Document's viewport is focused because the target is not focusable (step 4 of the algorithm)\n4. If the user press TAB key, focus moved to the logo (the top of page)\n5. The user needs to pressing and pressing TAB key to operate the target.\n\nIn case the target is focusable:\n1. A user opens https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#scroll-to-fragid:the-indicated-part-of-the-document\n2. \"When the user agent is required to scroll to the fragment identifier …\" is displayed (step 3 of the algorithm)\n3. “the indicated part of the document” is focused because the target is focusable (step 4 of the algorithm)\n4. If the user press TAB key, focus moved to “being rendered”.\n5. The user need to do nothing to operate the target and its siblings.\n\nAt this moment, some authors make all headings focusable to avoid this inconsistency.\nFor example, http://paypal.github.io/skipto/ adds `tabindex=\"-1\"`. If the spec fixed this inconsistency, authors can drop (some of) JavaScript stuffs.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
